[{:seon.task/name "a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"
  :seon.task/plan "Audits A, C #2, #16: a step that throws loses the request it took (Flow's step catch, jolt stdlib flow/impl.clj:488, keeps the old state and drops outputs), so callers grew deadlines with fake fallbacks ({} from root/state after 5 s, nil from web after 10 s, :timeout from fs-call!; root.clj:626,638,663,818, web.clj:130-136) and render re-asks after 2 s (render/proc.clj:76-85). The writer's catch answers only the current request and drops the queued batch (writer.clj:184-187). Each proc's entrant boundary answers every pending request with the one error map; then the deadlines and fallbacks are deleted and callers park on the reply."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-throwing-step-answers-every-pending-request
                      seon.procs-test/root-state-never-answers-an-empty-system}}
 {:seon.task/name "no-head-of-line-blocking"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit D F1 (reproduced): one busy agent plus one chatty agent stalled root's whole api ~25 s while health said :running: the api writes [agent :in] with Flow's blocking send (agent_proc.clj:19, 64 slots + 1 MB socket). The api never blocks on one agent (the stand-in holds overflow in its state and admits what fits); health reports progress (the oldest unanswered request), not liveness."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-busy-agent-never-stalls-another}}
 {:seon.task/name "shutdown-and-death-are-dataflow"
  :seon.task/plan "Audits A #2-3,#6-8, D F3-F5,F8: shutdown is four nested time bounds and two SIGKILL ladders with nothing recorded (root.clj:806-848, agent_proc.clj:42-56); child death is seen only by a 100 ms idle poll in the fork server (fork_server.clj:92-93), so deaths during a commit stream and after a failover are lost and a promoted server leaves a zombie. Child death is an event (kqueue EVFILT_PROC / SIGCHLD self-pipe in the fork server's poll); a death is recorded at socket EOF, its exit status added when known; spawn and clone are refused while closing; root parks on the drain; an agent that does not drain is a recorded problem; the conn and LMDB env belong to the writer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-death-is-recorded-at-once-and-its-status-added
                      seon.procs-test/shutdown-drains-without-a-kill}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-clock"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit A #13-16: the stall timer is a go block started inside the api's step (root.clj:449-457), the rate bucket a callback take! that forks an OS thread per pending tick in Jolt (fx.clj:93-104), the dashboard beat a raw thread (render/proc.clj:49-64). One declared :clock proc emits ticks on bounded channels; the stall and rate dials are named dragons."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-clock-is-the-only-time-source}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-threads-are-a-known-number"
  :seon.task/plan "bin/check lifecycle (10 start!/stop! cycles in one process) went red once on main (2026-10-21, 0b9df34, load ~8 with six lanes building): root's OS threads 25,25,25,26,26,29,29,29,29,29 against its bound of +2. Three reruns passed but drift the same way: 26->28, 25->27, flat 31. Steps then a plateau: a pool growing to a size that varies by run, not a leak per cycle. A thread count should be known: name each thread root starts (flow procs by workload, the fork-server reader, the flusher, the web/MCP listeners, any executor pool) and the number each owner owns, so the check asserts the exact count after each cycle instead of a growth bound. Find which pool grows and why its size depends on the run (lazy growth under contention?); fix it to a declared size, or record it as a declared, bounded pool. FOUND (lane THREADS): Jolt's threads stayed flat (42 across 9 cycles in one root) while the OS count grew by Chez's parallel-GC sweepers (sample: start_sweeper), 0 -> 5 -> 15: setup_sweepers (chez c/gc.c:2988) starts one per thread a collection finds waiting and keeps it, so the count follows load. Chez has no knob (parallel collection is automatic, release notes 10.0.0); our Chez (jolt/gc-sweepers) adds (cs)collect_sweepers n, and root fixes cfg/GC-SWEEPERS right after forking the fork server. Also: Jolt's runtime threads are named (jolt-carrier-N, jolt-timer, jolt-shutdown, ...); put!/take! that cannot complete are channel waiters run on fibers, not a thread each (Jolt c508734d); render is :mixed, not futurized onto the cached :compute pool; seon.root/declared-threads names every thread and lifecycle, scale and threads-declared assert the exact roster (seon.check/thread-diff)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares}}]
